window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"Start here","p":"Start here","u":"start.html","b":"ATI-OS is a ready-made Linux desktop: Arch and qtile, packaged. Install it once and you get the computer in the picture below, with 22 colour schemes and about forty-five tools already set up. Windows arrange themselves. Colours change everywhere at once. If you are stuck, start here. Black screen with text after installing — normal. Type letsgo . The desktop never appeared It starts, but looks completely plain Wi-Fi or Bluetooth says the service is not running Strange font, nothing lines up"},{"t":"Which install do I want?","p":"Start here","u":"start.html#which","b":"One question: do you already have Arch Linux on this computer? No. The computer is empty, or I want to start fresh Use a USB stick. It installs Linux and this desktop together. About 20 minutes. It erases the disk you point it at. Yes. Arch is already installed Run one command. It adds this desktop to what you have and erases nothing. One to two hours, almost all of it waiting. Both end at the same desktop."},{"t":"After it installs, you get a black screen","p":"Start here","u":"start.html#firstlogin","b":"This is normal. There is no login box, and the desktop does not start itself. Type your username, press Enter. Type your password, press Enter. The password stays invisible as you type. Not even dots. Keep going. Type letsgo and press Enter. The screen flickers. The desktop appears. If that says letsgo: command not found , type startx instead. More on this ."},{"t":"Words used on this site","p":"Start here","u":"start.html#what","b":"Word Meaning Arch Linux The version of Linux this runs on. It only works on Arch. tiling window manager Windows arrange themselves side by side. You do not drag or resize them with the mouse. qtile The particular tiling window manager used here. the bar The strip at the edge of the screen with the clock and battery. theme A set of colours. Changing it recolours everything at once, down to your folder icons. terminal A window where you type commands instead of clicking. TTY A plain text screen with no mouse. What you see before the desktop starts."},{"t":"What has not been tested","p":"Start here","u":"start.html#honest","b":"This is one person's desktop, shared. Some of it is proven on real machines. Some is not. Nobody has ever run this on: AMD or NVIDIA graphics. Only Intel has been seen working. The code handles all three, but two of them are untested. A 4K screen. The sizing maths is correct. Nobody has looked at the result. Some laptop battery indicators , on models that name them unusually. The USB installer also needs a computer that starts in UEFI mode . Almost anything built after 2012 does. Older machines are refused outright rather than half-installed. The built-in self-check can lie on a fresh machine."},{"t":"Once it is running","p":"Start here","u":"start.html#next","b":"Keyboard shortcuts The six you need on day one, then the rest. Changing the colours Two keys. 22 schemes. The bar and the popups What everything does when you click it. The tools you get A shelf for files, an update button, phone mirroring, dictation. Troubleshooting Real problems, most likely first. Where everything lives Which file to edit to change something. How it works The design reasoning, kept out of the way. Based on Distrotube's qtile configuration, extended with the author's own customisation and workflow. Every shortcut, command and path here was read out of the file it describes. "},{"t":"Install from a USB stick","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html","b":"Make a stick, start the computer from it, answer eight questions. About 20 minutes to install, plus 20 more on the first restart. This erases a disk. Everything on the disk you pick is gone and cannot be recovered. Copy anything you care about somewhere else first. Exception: if the computer already has another operating system and 25 GB of free space, the installer offers to sit alongside it. See question 1 ."},{"t":"What you need","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#need","b":"A USB stick 4 GB or larger. It gets erased. The target computer A disk of at least 30 GB, and UEFI mode. Internet on it A cable is easiest. Wi-Fi needs two extra commands."},{"t":"Which one do I want?","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#which-path","b":"Two ways in, and the one you want depends on what is on the computer already. Path 1 — USB stick Path 2 — Arch you have Who it is for A blank computer, or one you are willing to erase. A computer already running Arch Linux with no desktop on it. What it does Installs the whole operating system, disk and all. Adds the desktop to the Arch that is already there. Needs A 2.6 GB download and a USB stick. Nothing but the computer and a terminal. Erases A whole disk. Nothing. Takes About 20 minutes, plus 20 on the first restart. An hour or two, almost all of it waiting."},{"t":"Path 2 — Arch you already have","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#path-arch","b":"If the computer is already running Arch, none of the rest of this page applies. There is no image to fetch, no stick to write and no disk to erase. Clone the project and run the installer: git clone https://github.com/Mohamedattiadev/ATI-OS.git ~/.dotfiles \\ && cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts \\ && ./install.sh That is the whole of it. It asks for your password once and nothing else. Install onto Arch you already have is the full walkthrough: what it needs, what the 47 steps do, and what to do when one fails. Everything below is path 1."},{"t":"1. Make the stick","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#make","b":"Get the image, then write it to the stick."},{"t":"Download the image","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#download","b":"Most people should not build an operating system image. They should download one that somebody already built and check that it arrived intact. That is this path. The image is hosted on the Internet Archive — 2.6 GB in one file, no account, no mirror list. A single file on GitHub Releases is capped at 2 GB, which is why it is not there. The image ati-os-2026.08.04-x86_64.iso — 2.6 GB Its checksum ati-os-2026.08.04-x86_64.iso.sha256 — 95 bytes Where both live archive.org/details/ati-os-2026.08.04-x86_64 , which also carries a torrent Your download has been started. If nothing happened, use the i"},{"t":"An image ages","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#ageing","b":"The 31 prebuilt AUR packages inside it were compiled on the day it was built, and they are only accepted while they still match the Arch libraries they were compiled against. After roughly a year the installer stops trusting them and compiles from source on your machine instead — which is the correct thing to do, and turns a twenty-minute install into a two-hour one. It also raises the disk the install needs from 30 GB to 60, because build trees are large. Nothing breaks when that happens; the install just gets slow. If the published image has aged past that point you can build a fresh one, wh"},{"t":"Write it to the stick","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#write","b":"Downloaded or built, it is the same kind of file. Plug in the stick. Find out what it is called. lsblk Look for the size that matches. A 32 GB stick shows about 29.8G . The name looks like sdb . Write it down. Copy the image onto it. Replace sdX with that name, and if= with wherever you saved the download. sudo dd if=~/Downloads/ati-os-2026.08.04-x86_64.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress oflag=sync If you built the image yourself it is in ~/ati-os-build/out/ instead: sudo dd if=~/ati-os-build/out/ati-os-*.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress oflag=sync Check the name twice. This command do"},{"t":"2. Start the computer from it","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#boot","b":"Plug the stick into the target computer. Turn that computer fully off. Turn it on and tap the boot-menu key repeatedly. Dell, most Lenovo F12 HP F9 Asus, Acer F12 or Esc Anything else F8 , F10 , F11 , Esc A short list of things to start from appears. Pick your USB stick. Listed twice? Pick the UEFI one. UEFI is the modern way computers start. This installer supports nothing else. Pick wrong and it stops with a message rather than half-installing."},{"t":"3. The boot menu","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#menu","b":"Press Enter on the first entry: ATI-OS — Install (x86_64, UEFI) The others are a screen-reader version and a memory tester. Text scrolls for 15 to 60 seconds. Then a blue ATI-OS logo, a short welcome, and a prompt. On Wi-Fi? Connect now. The installer downloads software. A network cable already works. For Wi-Fi type iwctl and follow the prompts. For a mobile modem, use mmcli ."},{"t":"4. Start the installer","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#start","b":"ati-os-install The logo again, then \"Arch Linux, and a qtile desktop that is already configured.\" , then a section headed ── preflight ── ."},{"t":"5. It checks the computer","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#checks","b":"Six checks before anything is touched. Lines marked ok are good. A failure names exactly what is wrong and changes nothing. Check If it fails Started in UEFI mode Stops. Turn UEFI on in the firmware settings. Secure Boot is off Warns only. Do not ignore it. See below. The clock is right Warns. A wrong date makes downloaded software look untrustworthy, and the error will not say so. About 2 GB of memory Stops , and prints what it found. Internet works Stops , and prints the commands to connect. At least one disk Stops. Secure Boot. Turn it off in the firmware settings before continuing. Ignore "},{"t":"6. The questions","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#questions","b":"Eight, in this order. Square brackets show the default. Press Enter to take it."},{"t":"1. Which disk?","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-disk","b":"── target disk ── /dev/sda 238.5G Samsung SSD 860 /dev/sdb 57.3G SanDisk Ultra Disk to ERASE and install onto (e.g. /dev/sda): Type: the full name including /dev/ , such as /dev/sda . Means: this disk gets erased and gets the new system. Pick the one whose size matches the computer's built-in disk. Your USB stick is in the list, but you cannot pick it by accident. The installer recognises the stick it is running from and refuses it. If the disk already has another system With an existing operating system and 25 GB free, you get a choice: This disk already has an operating system on it. Existin"},{"t":"2. Hostname","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-host","b":"Hostname [ati-os]: Type: anything short with no spaces, or Enter for ati-os . Means: the name on your home network. Cosmetic."},{"t":"3. Username","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-user","b":"Username: Type: lowercase letters and digits. - and _ allowed. Must not start with a digit. Anything invalid and it asks again. Means: your account name. You type it at the text screen every time you start the computer. It also becomes the name in big block letters on the startup screen, so ati gives you ATI . This answer matters more than it looks The software list and settings profile are chosen by username , not by computer name. Two accounts on one machine can want different ones. See How it works ."},{"t":"4. Password","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-pass","b":"Password for <your name>: Repeat: Nothing appears as you type. No dots, no stars. Empty or mismatched and it asks again. The administrator account gets the same password."},{"t":"5. Time zone","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-tz","b":"Timezone [Europe/Istanbul]: Type: Enter if the guess is right. Otherwise Continent/City , like Europe/Berlin . Means: it sets the clock. The guess comes from your connection's apparent location. An unrecognised zone stops the installer rather than silently using the wrong one."},{"t":"6. Lock the disk with a password?","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-enc","b":"Encrypt the whole disk? Yes: a stolen machine reveals nothing. You type a passphrase at every boot, and if you forget it the data is GONE for good. Recommended for a laptop. Not needed for a desktop at home. Encrypt? [y/N]: Type: y , or Enter for no. Means: everything on the disk is scrambled, and you type an extra password every time you turn the computer on. A thief gets nothing. Forget that password and your files are gone permanently. No reset, no recovery. That is the entire point of it. Saying yes asks twice and requires at least 8 characters."},{"t":"7. Keyboard layout","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-kb","b":"Keyboard layout (see them all with: localectl list-keymaps) Keymap [us]: Type: Enter for a US keyboard, or the short code for yours: uk , de , fr , tr . Unrecognised codes are rejected and it asks again. Means: which letter appears when you press a key. Get it wrong and you will type your own password incorrectly at the first login with no way to see why. That is why you are asked."},{"t":"8. Language","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#q-locale","b":"System language (e.g. en_US.UTF-8, ar_EG.UTF-8, de_DE.UTF-8) Locale [en_US.UTF-8]: Type: Enter for English (US), or a code in that form. Means: the language of menus and messages. English is always set up as well, because a lot of software misbehaves confusingly without it."},{"t":"7. What you see while it works","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#working","b":"Nothing left to do. It prints a heading per stage. Heading What is happening ── partitioning /dev/sda ── Dividing up the disk. The point of no return. Anything failing after this leaves a half-installed disk, and the fix is always to run the installer again. ── mirrors ── Finding the fastest download servers. ── package signing keys ── Fetching the keys that prove downloads are genuine. ── installing the base system ── Basic Linux. The longest download. ── prebuilt AUR packages ── Copying in software compiled in advance. This is the time saving. ── configuring the system ── Clock, language, ke"},{"t":"8. Restart","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#restart","b":"Pull out the USB stick. Restart the computer. Do not leave the stick in. The computer may start from it again and show you the installer a second time."},{"t":"9. It builds the desktop","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#firstboot","b":"The desktop is not installed yet. It builds itself on this first restart. 47 steps scroll up the screen for about 20 minutes . Let it finish. Do not turn the computer off. Then you get a black screen with text, waiting for you to log in. If it failed , it says so and does not pretend otherwise. cat /var/log/ati-os-firstboot.log To try again: cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts ./wizard.sh Read the individual step errors, not the summary. On a fresh machine the step that reports a failure is almost never the step that caused it. One full disk once showed up as five unrelated broken steps."},{"t":"10. Log in","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#login","b":"No login box. Nothing starts by itself. Deliberate. Type your username, press Enter. Type your password, press Enter. The password stays invisible. Normal. Type letsgo and press Enter. The screen flickers. The desktop appears. If letsgo is not found, type startx . Two names for one job: letsgo only exists once a particular install step has succeeded."},{"t":"Done. What is left","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#after","b":"The desktop works. Three things need an account, and only you can make an account. All three are optional and can wait days. Thing Gives you Without it Password manager Your passwords on a keyboard shortcut No password menu. Nothing else changes. Note syncing Your to-do list on your phone The list still works locally. An AI key Extra suggestions in the translation and flashcard tools Those tools still work, with one section less. Setting each one up is on the other install page . The steps are the same whichever way you installed. Two extras are left out because most people do not want them: ."},{"t":"What this cannot do","p":"Install from a USB stick","u":"install-usb.html#limits","b":"UEFI only. Very old machines cannot. The stick still starts on them for rescue work, but installing is refused. It cannot shrink an existing Windows or Linux install. Free the space first. No AMD or NVIDIA graphics card has ever run this, and no 4K screen. The automated tests run inside a fake computer with no graphics hardware, so a passing test says nothing about how the animations will look on yours. The tests have never used a real USB stick. They start the image file directly. Troubleshooting → The USB stick lists every message the installer can refuse with. Every prompt quoted here is th"},{"t":"Install onto Arch you already have","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html","b":"One command adds the whole desktop to an Arch Linux computer you already have. It asks you nothing along the way."},{"t":"What you need","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#need","b":"Arch Linux Freshly installed, no desktop on it yet. 25 GB free On the disk. An hour or two Almost all waiting. You can walk away. Internet It erases nothing. Any settings file it would replace is moved to ~/DefaultConfig/<timestamp>/ first."},{"t":"1. Run the command","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#run","b":"Open a terminal, paste this in, press Enter. git clone https://github.com/Mohamedattiadev/ATI-OS.git ~/.dotfiles \\ && cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts \\ && ./install.sh It asks for your password once, near the start. Then a logo, then a list of steps, each with a spinner and a tick as it finishes. What does that command actually do? Three commands joined. git clone downloads the project to ~/.dotfiles . cd moves into it. ./install.sh runs the installer. && means \"only if the last one worked\". A failed download will not run the installer against nothing."},{"t":"2. Wait","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#wait","b":"47 steps run in order. Nothing for you to do. It will look frozen. It is not. One step compiles a piece of safety software from scratch and can sit there for close to an hour showing nothing. There is no faster way. A couple of other steps compile things too. Come back in an hour or two. You get a summary card at the end. You want 47 ok / 0 failed . A failed step is named, along with its error file at /tmp/wizard-<name>.err . Why it never asks you anything, and why that took work It is a guarantee, not a description. Every step runs with its input closed off, so it cannot stop and wait for you"},{"t":"3. Log in and start the desktop","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#login","b":"No login box. The desktop does not start by itself. Step 38 switches off every graphical login screen it can find. Log out, or restart. You land on a black screen with text on it — a TTY , a plain text screen with no mouse. Type your username, press Enter. Type your password, press Enter. The password does not appear as you type. Not even dots. That is normal. Type letsgo and press Enter. The screen flickers and the desktop appears. If it says letsgo: command not found , type startx instead. Why two different commands for one job? startx is the standard Linux command. letsgo is a helper this p"},{"t":"4. The three accounts","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#accounts","b":"The desktop is finished and working. What is left is three online accounts, and nobody can create an account for you. All three are optional and can wait days. Account What it gives you Without it Vaultwarden — a password manager A pop-up list of your passwords on Super + p then p No password menu. Nothing else changes. Simplenote — note syncing Your to-do list also appears in the Simplenote phone app The list still works. It is a file on your computer. Only the phone copy is missing. Gemini — an AI key Extra word suggestions and example sentences in the translation and flashcard tools All thr"},{"t":"Setting up the password manager","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#vaultwarden","b":"The installer already started the password server on your own computer and pointed the tools at it. Only the account is missing. Open a web browser and go to https://127.0.0.1:8222 A registration page appears. The s in https matters — without it the page refuses to load. Create an account with your email and a master password. Nothing here can choose the master password for you, and you must not forget it. Back on the desktop, press Super + p , then p . It asks for your email, once. Then it asks for your master password and keeps the list unlocked for 15 minutes. The server runs on your own co"},{"t":"Setting up note syncing","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#simplenote","b":"Do this after your first desktop login. It may need a web browser. cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts ./wizard.sh --only=simplenote There is no --yes on that command, on purpose. Without it the step is allowed to ask you questions, which is what you want here. It asks for your Simplenote email and password, then proves it worked by sending your to-do note for real. Some networks block the login server while the notes themselves stay reachable. It notices, copies a one-line snippet to your clipboard to paste into your browser, and takes a login token that way. That is the part needing a browser."},{"t":"Setting up the AI key","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#gemini","b":"Open the file ~/.config/secrets.env and put your key in it: GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here The file already exists, with instructions inside and the key left blank. Only you can read it, and it is never uploaded."},{"t":"Optional extras","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#extras","b":"The install leaves out software unrelated to the desktop. Skipping it changes nothing about how the desktop looks or works. Add it whenever you want it: ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/wizard.sh --yes --only=dcli-sync-extra Containers docker · docker-buildx · docker-compose Extra developer tools github-cli · git-lfs · clang · jdk17-openjdk · uv · ruff Virtual machines qemu-desktop · edk2-ovmf Printing cups · cups-pk-helper · system-config-printer Diagnostics xorg-server-xephyr · mesa-utils Safe to run again. Anything already installed is skipped. Caps Lock as an extra Alt key ./wizard.sh --yes --on"},{"t":"What the 47 steps actually do","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#modules","b":"You do not need this to install. It is here so a failed step means something. Show all 47 steps, in the order they run # Name What it does 1 sanity Checks this really is Arch, and that the files are where it expects 2 bootstrap Installs the handful of tools the installer itself needs 3 yay Installs the helper that fetches community software 4 dcli Installs the tool that keeps the software list in sync 5 stow Puts the settings files in place. Anything it would overwrite is moved to ~/DefaultConfig/<timestamp>/ 6 arch-config Points the software list at your username 7 paths Fills your home folde"},{"t":"Doing less than all of it","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#options","b":"Mostly for when one step failed and you want to re-run only that one. ./wizard.sh # pick steps from a list, with checkboxes ./wizard.sh --dry-run # show every command, change nothing ./wizard.sh --yes # exactly the same as ./install.sh ./wizard.sh --only=themes # run one step (or several, comma-separated) ./wizard.sh --skip=whisper # run everything except these ./wizard.sh --audit # compare what should be installed against what is ./wizard.sh --uninstall # undo the settings changes The = is required. --only themes with a space is rejected outright rather than ignored. An ignored filter would r"},{"t":"What has not been tested","p":"Install onto Arch you already have","u":"install-git.html#limits","b":"AMD and NVIDIA graphics cards. Only Intel has been seen working. The driver choice for the other two is reasoned from documentation, not observed. Very high resolution (4K) screens. The sizing maths has been checked and is correct; nobody has looked at the result on a real 4K screen. Some laptop battery indicators , on models that name them unusually. Proven on a clean test machine: all 47 steps complete, the desktop starts, and it loads these settings instead of silently falling back to a default. On the author's own hardware, also the startup screen and the window animations. validate.sh can"},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html","b":"This desktop is driven from the keyboard. Windows arrange themselves; you move between them with keys."},{"t":"The six for day one","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#day-one","b":"Learn these and you can use the computer. Everything else can wait. Press this To do this Super + Enter Open a terminal Super + Shift + Enter Open a program. A search box appears; type a few letters of the name and press Enter Super + Shift + c Close the window you are looking at Super + j / Super + k Move to the next / previous window Super + 1 … Super + 9 Switch to workspace 1…9. Nine separate screens' worth of windows Super + Shift + k then k Show the shortcut list on screen. When you forget one, this is faster than this page"},{"t":"Which key is which","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#keys","b":"Written here as The key on your keyboard Super The Windows key , between Ctrl and Alt. On an Apple keyboard, the Command key. Alt The Alt key , exactly as labelled. Super + Shift + c Hold Super and Shift together, then tap c . Super + P then c Hold Super and tap P . Let go. Then tap c on its own. Caps Lock is not an Alt key unless you asked for it. An optional extra makes it one, but it is not installed by default. Use your real Alt key. To turn it on: ./wizard.sh --yes --only=xmodmap . It takes Caps Lock away completely. There is no way to still get capital-lock."},{"t":"Moving between windows","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#windows","b":"Press Result Super + j Move to the window below Super + k Move to the window above Super + h Move to the window on the left Super + l Move to the window on the right Super + Shift + h Move the window itself to the left Super + Shift + l Move the window itself to the right Super + Shift + j Move the window itself down Super + f Make the window fill the whole screen. Press again to undo Super + x Make the window as big as it can be without hiding the others. Press again to undo Super + t Let this one window float freely, so you can drag it. Press again to put it back in the grid Super + Tab Chan"},{"t":"Workspaces","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#groups","b":"Nine workspaces, like nine separate desks. Each holds its own windows. Switching is instant. The active workspace's icon takes the accent colour, and the window list beside it refills with that workspace's windows. Press Result Super + 1 … 9 Go to that workspace Super + Shift + 1 … 9 Send the current window to that workspace Programs open on a workspace chosen for them, so things stay tidy on their own. Workspace Opens here 1 TickTick 2 qutebrowser, Zen Browser, VLC, Firefox 3 File managers 4 VS Code, Zed, the terminal, Cursor 5 Brave 6 Chrome, Chromium 7 Empty — yours to use 8 Documents: Libr"},{"t":"Opening programs","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#launch","b":"These are toggles, not plain launchers. Already running, they jump you to it. Not running, they start it. Press Program Super + Enter A terminal (kitty) Super + Shift + Enter The program launcher. Type a name, press Enter Super + n Terminal Super + b Brave browser Super + v qutebrowser Super + m File manager Super + Shift + t Telegram Super + Shift + o Obsidian, for drawing (assumes you have Excalidraw installed in it) Super + Shift + s Your notes file, in a terminal editor Alt + Shift + a Anki flashcards"},{"t":"Whole-computer shortcuts","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#session","b":"Press Result Super + Shift + r Restart the desktop. Your windows stay where they are. Do this after changing a setting Super + Shift + q Log out, shut down, restart or lock. A menu appears Super + Shift + F5 Refresh the desktop without restarting the computer Super + Shift + F6 Show your Android phone's screen on the computer Super + Shift + z Move the bar between the top and the bottom of the screen Super + ` Show or hide the second group of bar icons Print Screenshot. Drag a box; it goes straight to your clipboard F8 Dictation, live. Speak, and the words type themselves as you pause. Press a"},{"t":"The Alt shortcuts","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#alt","b":"Press Result Alt + Shift + d The shelf. A panel slides down that you can drag files, text and images into, and drag back out later. See qdrop Alt + v Clipboard history. Everything you have copied recently, with picture previews. Ctrl + j and Ctrl + k move up and down Alt + p Today and this week's plans and to-dos Alt + n Dismiss the notification on screen Alt + Tab Show or hide the system tray icons Alt + ` Show or hide the first group of bar icons"},{"t":"Clicking without a mouse","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#hintium","b":"Press one of these and letters appear over everything clickable. Type the letters on the one you want and it is clicked. Works in browsers, menus and most applications. This is Hintium , installed into ~/.local/share/hintium by the installer. If the labels never appear, run hintium --doctor — it checks every library, typelib and browser flag it needs, and prints the command to fix whatever is missing. Press Result Alt + Space Label everything clickable and click the one you pick Alt + j Pick a scrollable area and scroll it with the keyboard Alt + / Search on-screen text, then click the match A"},{"t":"Drop-down windows","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#scratch","b":"These slide down over whatever you are doing. The same key sends them away. Press Window Alt + 1 A terminal Alt + 2 A second terminal Alt + 5 A calculator Alt + 8 WhatsApp Alt + 9 DeepSeek Alt + 0 ChatGPT Alt + 3 and Alt + 4 still mean sound and monitors, but they open the sound and monitor popups rather than a window. Why the three web ones cost almost nothing WhatsApp, DeepSeek and ChatGPT are separate profiles inside the one Brave you already have, not three browsers. Three browsers each spawn their own graphics process, network service, storage service and three helper processes to render "},{"t":"Mouse","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#mouse","b":"Do this Result Hold Super and drag with the left button Move a floating window Hold Super and drag with the right button Resize a floating window Hold Super and click the middle button Bring the window to the front Windows focus when the mouse moves over them. No click needed."},{"t":"Modes","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#modes","b":"A mode puts the keyboard into a temporary state. A coloured chip appears in the bar naming the mode and the keys it accepts. Esc always leaves. One chip, six modes: rofi, language, resize, draw, cheatsheet, media. Each carries its own colour and spells out the keys it takes. Clips on this page are cropped to that corner. Press Mode Stays open? Super + P The menu key . Opens everything else No. One key, one action, then it ends Super + P then w Wallpaper picker Yes Super + P then n Wi-Fi Yes Super + P then b Bluetooth Yes Alt + 3 Sound Yes. Same key closes it Alt + 4 Monitors Yes. Same key clos"},{"t":"Super+P — the menu key","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#rofi-mode","b":"The one to remember. Hold Super , tap P , let go, tap one letter. Almost everything is one letter from here. Then press What you get c Change the colour theme w Change the wallpaper n Connect to Wi-Fi b Connect Bluetooth p Your saved passwords l Screen brightness t Your to-do list k Close a frozen program a Add an Anki flashcard e Translate the text you have selected s Spell check i Screenshot, with annotation tools r Record video, audio, webcam or a gif d Open one of your documents f Edit a settings file m Read a manual page o Store and copy notes y YouTube menu h List every bundled menu scri"},{"t":"Wallpaper picker — Super+P then w","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#wallpaper-mode","b":"Press Result h / l Move left / right j / k Move down / up r Jump to a random one / Search by name Enter Use this one q or Esc Close Drop any image into ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/ and it appears here straight away. Changing the wallpaper does not change your colours , unless you are on the theme called Wallpaper . On every other theme you chose those colours deliberately, so a new picture is only a new picture. See Changing the colours ."},{"t":"Wi-Fi — Super+P then n","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#wifi-mode","b":"Press Result j / k Move down / up the list g / Shift + g Jump to the top / bottom Enter Connect d Disconnect x Forget this network n Connect to a hidden network t Turn Wi-Fi off or on s Show a QR code so a phone can join this network r Search for networks again / Search the list c Give up on a connection that is taking too long q or Esc Close A wrong password makes it ask again rather than saving a network that can never connect."},{"t":"Bluetooth — Super+P then b","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#bt-mode","b":"Searching, pairing, connecting, disconnecting and removing all happen here. Press Result j / k Move down / up the list g / Shift + g Jump to the top / bottom Enter Connect d Disconnect x Remove this device t Turn Bluetooth off or on r Search for devices / Search the list c Give up on a connection that is hanging. Connecting to a device out of range otherwise never finishes q or Esc Close"},{"t":"Sound — Alt+3","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#audio-mode","b":"Choose speakers or headphones, set per-program volume, pick a microphone. Alt + 3 again closes it. Press Result j / k Move down / up g / Shift + g Jump to the top / bottom Tab / Shift + Tab Next / previous view o Outputs — speakers and headphones i Inputs — microphones a What is playing right now, per program s What is recording right now p Sound card modes Shift + p Sockets — the setting that picks headphones over speakers on one card Shift + c Every sound card at once Enter Use this one. On an output this also moves whatever is already playing across — otherwise only new sounds would follow "},{"t":"Monitors — Alt+4","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#display-mode","b":"Set up a second screen: position, resolution, which is primary. Alt + 4 again closes it. Press Result i Laptop screen only e External screen only m Show the same thing on both h / l Put this screen to the left / right of the other u / d Put it above / below j / k Move down / up. While arranging, these move the screen instead of the cursor g / Shift + g Jump to the top / bottom Tab Select the next screen a Start arranging them = While arranging: line them up by top edges, middles, or bottom edges Enter Open the resolution list for this screen, and apply from inside it Backspace Go back p Make t"},{"t":"Volume and brightness — Super+/","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#media-mode","b":"Press Result Shift + k / Shift + j Volume up / down Shift + m Mute Shift + l / Shift + h Screen brighter / dimmer Shift + p Shrink the video you are watching into a corner, so you can work over it 1 … 9 Switch workspace q or Esc Leave"},{"t":"Resizing windows — Super+r","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#resize-mode","b":"Press Result Shift + l Make the main window wider Shift + h Make it narrower Shift + n Put everything back to the default sizes 1 … 9 Switch workspace q or Esc Leave"},{"t":"Drawing on the screen — Super+Shift+w","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#draw-mode","b":"Draw over anything on screen — useful when explaining something on a call. Press Result w Start or stop drawing. While it is on, drag the mouse to draw c Erase everything z Undo r Redo v Hide or show what you drew 1 … 9 Switch workspace q or Esc Leave"},{"t":"Hint mode — Super+Shift+f","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#hint-mode","b":"The same six mouse-free actions as the Alt shortcuts , in one place. Each one ends the mode. Press Result h Label everything clickable, and click the one you pick s Pick a scrollable area and scroll it with the keyboard f Search on-screen text — numbers pick, Enter clicks v Move a text cursor with keys. v selects, y copies Shift + v Type a word to find, and land the cursor there e Edit this text box in a proper editor. Save and quit puts it back t Scroll to the top, fast b Scroll to the bottom, fast 1 … 9 Switch workspace q or Esc Leave Why every action ends the mode It used to stay open so ac"},{"t":"Switching keyboard language — Super+Space","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#lang-mode","b":"Press Layout e English a Arabic t Turkish d German q or Esc Leave These four are tied to positions on the keyboard, not letters. They stay on the same physical keys whatever language is active. That matters, because you press them precisely when the keyboard is producing the wrong letters. The letters listed are US-keyboard positions."},{"t":"The built-in cheatsheets — Super+Shift+k","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#cheat-mode","b":"Every shortcut on cards, on screen. Faster than this web page. Press Result k Show the desktop shortcuts. If a sheet is already open, scroll up j Scroll down v Show the Vim text editor shortcuts instead f Show the shell and terminal shortcuts instead Tab Next screenful Shift + Tab Previous screenful 1 … 9 Switch workspace q or Esc Close Only one sheet is open at a time, so j and k always mean the one you can see. Modifier keys appear as symbols: ⇧ Shift, ⌃ Ctrl, ⏎ Enter, ␣ Space. The header carries the key to them."},{"t":"Passthrough — Super+F12","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#passthrough","b":"Turns this desktop's shortcuts off temporarily so a program can receive keys the desktop would otherwise take: a virtual machine, a remote desktop, a game. Super + F12 again, or Esc , turns them back on."},{"t":"Getting this list on screen","p":"Keyboard shortcuts","u":"keybindings.html#live","b":"The desktop builds this list from the settings it is running, so it is never out of date: Press Super + Shift + k then k . Or click the logo in the bar and choose Keybindings . Or type qtile-keys in a terminal. qtile-keys lists only shortcuts carrying a written description. Several real ones deliberately have none: the popup navigation keys, the drop-down windows, the language keys. Its count is smaller than the tables here. This page includes them all. Every shortcut here was read out of the live configuration file. None were guessed. Next: changing the colours →"},{"t":"Changing the colours","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html","b":"Pick a colour scheme and everything follows: the bar, the terminal, the menus, the notifications, the browser, your text editor, your folder icons."},{"t":"How to change it","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#how","b":"Press Super + P , let go, then press c . A list of colour scheme names appears. The one you are using now has a ● next to it. Move up and down with the arrow keys, and press Enter on the one you want. The screen goes soft and frosted for a second or two while everything recolours, then comes back in the new colours. Or type the name in a terminal: theme-apply gruvbox Clicking through the list quickly is fine. A new choice cancels the one still in progress."},{"t":"The 22 colour schemes","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#list","b":"Ten of them. There are 22 in total. Name Name Name doomone (the default) kanagawa cyberpunk-neon dracula oxocarbon synthwave gruvbox onedark matrix nord palenight mono-dark tokyonight nightowl mono-light (the only light one) catppuccin github-dark wal — shown as Wallpaper in the picker monokai ayu-mirage everforest rose-pine"},{"t":"If you want a light theme","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#light","b":"mono-light is the one light scheme. Everything else is dark. It also switches the underlying window style and icon set to their light versions, so ordinary applications render properly dark-on-white instead of looking like a dark theme with pale colours poured over it."},{"t":"Matching your wallpaper","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#wal","b":"The scheme called Wallpaper in the picker ( wal if you are typing) reads the colours out of your current wallpaper and builds a scheme from them. theme-apply wal Drop any image into ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/ . It appears in the wallpaper picker ( Super + P then w ) immediately. Colours are worked out the first time you use it and remembered afterwards. Nothing to run. How the colours get picked, and why they are always readable Colours pulled straight out of a photo are often unreadable. Grey text on a grey bar. So every generated scheme is forced to meet a quality bar first: the strongest access"},{"t":"Wallpaper and theme are separate","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#wallpaper-vs-theme","b":"Changing your wallpaper does not change your colours , unless you are on the Wallpaper scheme. On any other scheme you chose those colours deliberately, so a new picture changes only the picture. True whichever of the three ways you use to set one."},{"t":"What changes colour","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#retints","b":"Almost everything. In particular: The bar, and all the popup windows The terminal — already-open ones too, immediately Every menu and picker Notifications Ordinary application windows, and Qt applications like Telegram and the file manager The web browsers' own toolbars and tabs — Brave, Chrome, Chromium, qutebrowser The Neovim text editor Your folder icons The mouse pointer theme The startup screen — with one extra step Two things need a moment. Telegram and the file manager pick up new colours when they next start, not while running. Close and reopen them, or log out and back in. Web pages a"},{"t":"The startup screen","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#splash","b":"Your name in block letters, with a small Arch logo underneath and three dots near the bottom of the screen that light up as the machine starts — all in the current colours. Nothing to set up. The name comes from your username, so ati becomes ATI . The dots do two jobs: a wave travels through them so a slow start never looks like a stuck one, and they fill left to right as the machine actually gets going. This one does not change with the rest. It has to be rebuilt, which takes about a minute. Far too slow for a colour change. So a theme change tells you the startup screen is out of date and yo"},{"t":"If something stays the old colour","p":"Changing the colours","u":"themes.html#trouble","b":"What you see What to do Telegram or the file manager is still the old colours Close and reopen it. Those read their colours once, when they start. The startup screen is still the old colours Run boot-splash sync . See above . Folder icons never change One optional package is missing. It fails silently by design rather than breaking the theme change. The web page content is not dark That is deliberate — see above . Everything looks wrong and the text does not line up A font is missing. See Troubleshooting . Something else Troubleshooting → Colours lists every case that has come up. Every scheme"},{"t":"The bar and the popups","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html","b":"The bar is the strip along the edge of your screen. Almost everything in it does something when clicked."},{"t":"Start by clicking the logo","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#logo","b":"Left-click the logo. It opens a menu answering \"what can this computer do?\". It is the fastest way to find anything. Esc goes up a level rather than closing the menu, so you can look around without reopening it. Click What happens Left The documentation menu Middle A terminal Right The program launcher The menu has seven sections: Section What it gives you Keybindings Every keyboard shortcut, searchable. Type a few letters to filter Cheatsheets Opens the on-screen shortcut cards for the desktop, Vim, or the shell Documentation Reads the manuals — nicely formatted, not raw text, and read-only s"},{"t":"Everything else in the bar","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#chips","b":"Click this And you get The camera Screenshot — drag a box, it goes to your clipboard. Same as pressing Print The CPU or memory number A full-screen view of what is using your computer The updates count The update window — see qupdate The disk indicator How full your disks are The battery Charge level and whether it is charging. Only appears if the computer has a battery The keyboard language Switches to the next language The clock Today's and this week's plans and to-dos The media controls Left click: play or pause. Right click: previous track The ✖ icon Opens the wallpaper picker, or closes i"},{"t":"Hidden groups of icons","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#boxes","b":"The bar cannot fit everything. Some icons live in groups you open when you want them. Press Shows Alt + ` The first group of system icons Super + ` The second group Alt + Tab The system tray — icons put there by running programs Opening a keyboard mode closes whichever group is open and remembers it, so the mode's chip never fights for space."},{"t":"The coloured chip","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#modechip","b":"Press a mode shortcut and a coloured chip appears in the bar. It names the mode and lists the keys it accepts. Each mode has its own colour. That chip answers \"which keys work right now?\". If the keyboard seems to have stopped responding, look there. You are probably in a mode. Esc always leaves."},{"t":"Moving the bar","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#move","b":"Press Super + Shift + z to move the bar between the top and the bottom of the screen."},{"t":"The popup windows","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#popups","b":"Drawn by the desktop itself, not separate programs. Keyboard-driven, and they close when you leave. The sound popup. Every output with its volume, balance and profile; Tab across to the microphones. Popup What it is for Open it with Sound Choose speakers or headphones, set the volume of individual programs, pick a microphone Alt + 3 Monitors Set up a second screen: where it sits, its resolution, which is primary Alt + 4 Wi-Fi Connect to a network Super + P then n QR code Share your Wi-Fi with a phone by scanning s , inside the Wi-Fi popup Bluetooth Pair and connect devices Super + P then b Wal"},{"t":"The cheatsheets","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#cheatsheets","b":"Super + Shift + k then k . Every shortcut on a card, with the key on the right. They scroll : j and k move a few rows, Tab and Shift + Tab move a whole screenful, and the header shows how far down you are. v switches to the Vim sheet and f to the shell one. Esc closes. Modifier keys are drawn as symbols: ⇧ Shift, ⌃ Ctrl, ⏎ Enter, ␣ Space. The header carries the key to them. Why they scroll instead of paging Paging tied how much you could see to how big the window was. The only way to show more shortcuts was to be bigger, and they had grown to nearly the size of a 1366×768 screen, covering the "},{"t":"The welcome tour","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#tour","b":"On your first login after installing, a short tour appears: welcome, workspaces, the shortcuts worth knowing first, finish. It runs once . After that, open it from the tooltip icon in the bar."},{"t":"Why a restart does not flicker","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#veil","b":"Restarting the desktop with Super + Shift + r , or changing your theme, would normally mean watching every window from every workspace flash across the screen for two seconds. Instead a frosted overlay covers the screen. It shows a card per window with its real icon, and a progress indicator reporting genuine progress rather than a timer. Your layout survives. Window sizes, which workspace each window is on, and which was focused all come back as they were. Windows you moved by hand stay where you put them rather than jumping back to their default workspace."},{"t":"If everything is too small or too big","p":"The bar and the popups","u":"widgets.html#scaling","b":"The desktop measures your screen at login and sizes text, bar and icons to suit. Disagree with the result? Two clicks, not a settings file: ui-scale-toggle That opens a picker. Also in the logo menu under Appearance → UI scale . Or set it directly: ui-scale --set 1.25 # pin a value; it survives re-detection ui-scale --auto # go back to measuring automatically ui-scale --show # what was detected, what is pinned, what is active It re-measures at every login, so plugging in an external monitor adjusts by itself. Nobody has looked at this on a real 4K screen. The sizing has been checked mathematic"},{"t":"The tools you get","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html","b":"About forty-five small commands come with this desktop. These are the ones you will use."},{"t":"All of them at a glance","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#glance","b":"Tool What it is for How to open it The shelf A panel you drag files into now and drag out later Alt + Shift + d The updater Update or install software with checkboxes, no typing Click the updates count in the bar Passwords A pop-up list of your saved passwords Super + p then p Phone mirroring Your Android phone's screen on the computer, controllable Super + Shift + F6 Dictation Speak, and the words are typed F8 or F9 Clipboard history Everything you copied recently, with picture previews Alt + v Screenshots Drag a box, straight to the clipboard Print Screen recording Video, audio, webcam or a "},{"t":"The shelf","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#qdrop","b":"A panel slides down from the top of the screen. Drag files, text, links or images into it. They stay. Later, in another program, drag them back out. It solves \"I want to attach this file to that email, but they are on different workspaces\". Using it Do this Result Alt + Shift + d Show or hide it Shake the mouse while dragging a file It comes to you. Any direction. This is the good one Drop something in It is added. Links are recognised automatically Drag an item out Paste it into any program Ctrl + V Paste from the clipboard — text, files, or an image you copied from a web page Ctrl + F Search"},{"t":"Updating without a terminal","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#qupdate","b":"Update and install software by ticking boxes. Click the updates count in the bar. A window opens with two tabs. The Updates tab Everything with a new version. Each row: a checkbox, a badge saying where it comes from, the old and new version numbers. Two buttons at the bottom: Update selected — only the ones you ticked. Full upgrade — everything. Use this one normally. It snapshots your system first, so a bad update can be undone. Tick Run in background and no terminal appears. You get a notification when it finishes either way. The Install tab Type a name. Results are sorted so the one you mea"},{"t":"Passwords","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#passwords","b":"Your passwords in a pop-up list, on a keyboard shortcut. Nothing leaves your computer. Create the account first . Two minutes, once. Then press Super + p , let go, press p . Type to filter. Press Result Enter Copy the password. Your clipboard is wiped 30 seconds later Alt + u Copy the username Alt + t Copy the two-factor code Alt + o Open that site in your browser Alt + a Type the password into the window you came from. It never touches the clipboard Alt + n New entry — it can generate a 24-character password for you Alt + e Edit an entry Alt + x Delete an entry Alt + s Sync now The hint line "},{"t":"Your phone on the screen","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#phone","b":"Your Android phone's screen in a window, controlled with your mouse and keyboard. Sound comes out of the computer's speakers. The first time On the phone, turn on Wireless debugging : Settings → Developer options → Wireless debugging. This survives restarts, so it really is a one-time thing. On the computer, press Super + Shift + F6 . A window opens with a QR code and a text box. Either scan the QR code with the phone (Wireless debugging → Pair device with QR code ), or tap Pair device with pairing code and type the 6 digits into the text box. Whichever finishes first wins. After that the shor"},{"t":"Dictation","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#dictation","b":"Speak instead of typing. It runs entirely on your own computer. Nothing is sent anywhere. Press What happens F8 Live. Start talking. Each phrase is typed the moment you pause. Press F8 again to stop F9 All at once. Start talking, press F9 again when you are done, and the whole thing is typed out F8 for a chat message. F9 for a paragraph you want to think through first. Transcribing nonsense? Your microphone volume was probably reset to a distorting level. A background service fixes that, installed as part of a normal install. See Troubleshooting ."},{"t":"Notes and to-dos","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#notes","b":"Press What you get Super + Shift + s Your main notes file, in an editor. Press again to hide it Alt + p , or the clock Today's and this week's plans and to-dos Super + P then t A to-do manager Super + P then o Store and copy short notes With note syncing set up , your to-do list also appears in the Simplenote phone app. Saving uploads. Opening the window downloads. Editing in both places is safe. Change the list on both and it will not guess a winner. You get a second file with a timestamp in its name holding the phone's version, and you merge by hand."},{"t":"The menus behind Super+P","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#menus","b":"All searchable lists. Type to filter, arrows to move, Enter to choose. Press What it does Super + P k Close a frozen program. Shows what is running with its window title, so you can tell which browser tab is the problem Super + P l Screen brightness Super + P e Translate the selected text, with a dictionary table Super + P s Spell and grammar check a word or a sentence Super + P a Build an Anki flashcard and send it to Anki Super + P i Screenshot, then annotate it before saving Super + P r Record video, audio, webcam or a gif Super + P d Find and open one of your documents Super + P f Browse a"},{"t":"Updating safely","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#updating","b":"Use the updater window , or type: dcli update Not a plain pacman -Syu . Three safety nets sit around the proper command: Refused if your disk is nearly full. Applies to any tool, so it cannot be worked around by accident. Refused if your safety snapshots sit somewhere that would not survive the problem they are for. After an update, anything that broke gets the repair command put straight on your clipboard. If an update breaks something Work down. Each step is a bigger hammer than the last. Put the broken piece of software back to its previous version. Restart, and choose the fallback kernel f"},{"t":"A few more","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#more","b":"Type this To do this letsgo or startx Start the desktop from the text screen theme-apply gruvbox Change the colour theme ui-scale-toggle Change the size of everything boot-splash preview See the startup screen without restarting ati-update Get the latest version of this desktop. See below hintium --doctor Check why the mouse-free clicking modes are not working qtile-keys Print every keyboard shortcut fc-cache -fv Notice newly installed fonts qtile cmd-obj -o cmd -f reload_config Reload settings without a full restart All bundled commands work from anywhere. ls /usr/local/bin shows the complete"},{"t":"Keeping up to date","p":"The tools you get","u":"tools.html#update","b":"The USB image is built now and then. The desktop itself is improved continuously. You do not need a new USB stick to get those improvements — one command brings your machine level with the latest version: ati-update It shows you what changed and asks before doing anything. It works out for itself what a given update actually needs — new settings linked into place, newly required programs installed, the boot screen regenerated, the desktop restarted — and does only those. Your windows survive the restart. Variation What it does ati-update --check Say what would happen, change nothing ati-update"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html","b":"Find what you are seeing in the headings below. Every problem here is real, with the cause and the fix."},{"t":"The first five minutes","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#first","b":""},{"t":"I get a black screen with text on it, and nothing happens","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#black-screen","b":"Nothing is wrong. There is no graphical login screen, and the desktop does not start itself. Deliberate. Type your username, press Enter. Type your password, press Enter. The password does not appear as you type. Not even dots. Keep going. Type letsgo and press Enter. If that says letsgo: command not found , type startx . Same job. letsgo only exists once a particular install step has succeeded."},{"t":"It says \"Server is already active for display 0\"","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#x-active","b":"A previous session left a marker file behind. letsgo clears it for you. With startx , do it by hand: First check whether the desktop is genuinely already running: pgrep -x Xorg If that prints a number, the desktop is running. Switch to it with Ctrl + Alt + F1 and stop here. If it printed nothing, remove the leftover and try again: rm -f /tmp/.X0-lock startx"},{"t":"The desktop never appeared after installing from USB","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#desktop-missing","b":"The desktop builds itself on the first restart, not during the install. About 20 minutes, with progress on screen. A failure is reported, and it retries next time rather than pretending it worked. cat /var/log/ati-os-firstboot.log To run it again yourself: cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts ./wizard.sh"},{"t":"The desktop starts but looks completely plain","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#stock-qtile","b":"No colours, no bar, nothing like the pictures. The settings file had an error, so the window manager fell back to its built-in default. Silently. bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/validate.sh That loads the settings for real and reports the error. Also worth reading: grep -i config ~/.local/share/qtile/qtile.log | head"},{"t":"The self-check says everything passed, but nothing works","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#validate-lies","b":"validate.sh marks anything it cannot check as \"skipped\" and still reports success. On a bad install it skips everything and finishes green. Trust the installer's own count: 47 ok / 0 failed ."},{"t":"One rule before you start digging","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#rule","b":"The step that reports a failure is almost never the step that caused it. One full disk once showed up as five broken steps, 32 steps after the real cause. One blocked software transaction showed up as two. Read the individual error files, not the summary card: ls /tmp/wizard-*.err cat /tmp/wizard-<name>.err That is what separated five real causes from twenty-two symptoms."},{"t":"During the install","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#install","b":"It finished, but some software is missing The software sync prints \"✓ Packages synced successfully\" whatever happened above it. Check yourself: dcli sync --dry-run That lists anything still missing. Three causes have produced it: Admin permission expired part-way through. Things were built and never installed. The installer now keeps it alive for the whole run. Two pieces of software conflicted. It is one transaction, so everything in that round failed, not just the two that clashed. One package's build tools were not ready. Breaks only the packages needing them. It ran out of disk space About"},{"t":"The USB stick","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#atios","b":"The computer will not start from the stick at all Three things, in this order: Pick the UEFI entry. The boot menu usually lists the same stick twice. Choose the one that says UEFI. Turn Secure Boot off in the computer's firmware settings. Check the copy actually finished. Copying to a USB stick can return before the data is really there. sha256sum -c ati-os-*.iso.sha256 \"No bootable device\" after the install finished Almost always Secure Boot. The installer uses an unsigned startup program, which Secure Boot refuses to run. The install completes perfectly, then the computer will not start. It "},{"t":"The desktop itself","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#desktop","b":"Every shortcut that opens a program suddenly fails Browsers, terminal, file manager, Telegram. All of them. The log shows an error mentioning cmd_spawn . Cause: an update to the window manager renamed something the settings file uses. Fix: update this project's files, or edit the settings to use the new name. I changed a setting and nothing happened Press Super + Shift + r . Your windows stay where they are. A lighter \"reload\" command exists, but only a full restart reliably picks up new software and new settings. The bar shows an error where the battery should be On a desktop with no battery."},{"t":"Colours","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#theming","b":"Telegram or the file manager is still the old colour They read their colours once, at start. Close and reopen them. Still wrong? Log out and back in. The startup screen is still the old colour That one has to be rebuilt, which takes about a minute: boot-splash sync Folder icons never change colour An optional package is missing. It fails silently by design rather than breaking the whole theme change. Web pages are not dark Deliberate. Rather than a filter over the top, the desktop tells websites you prefer dark mode, so each serves its own dark design. For a site with none, turn on force-dark "},{"t":"Text looks wrong","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#fonts","b":"Everything is in a strange font and nothing lines up What you see: the bar, popups and notifications in an odd font, with every column ragged. Nothing reports an error. It looks like broken layout code. What it is: a missing font. The system never reports one. It silently substitutes, and here the substitute is a Chinese/Japanese/Korean face. This project lines its columns up with spaces, and spaces only line up in a fixed-width font. Check it: fc-match \"JetBrainsMono Nerd Font\" It should answer with that same name. Anything else means the font is missing. bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/valid"},{"t":"Wi-Fi and Bluetooth","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#network","b":"The pickers say the service is not running What you see: Super + P then n or b opens and immediately errors, or the Bluetooth list never fills. The tray icons do nothing. What it is: installing software is not the same as switching it on. Fix: ./wizard.sh --yes --only=radios To check afterwards: systemctl is-enabled NetworkManager.service bluetooth.service Watch out for service_trim.sh . That optional tuning script offers to switch Bluetooth off. Say yes and the Bluetooth picker goes dead with no other symptom. Connecting hangs forever The busy indicator spins and never stops. Press c to give "},{"t":"The menus","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#menus","b":"Pressing Enter in the password list does nothing The list opens, the entries are there, Enter closes it and copies nothing. In the full file, with two other password-tool problems. Every confirmation answers \"cancel\" by itself Only with more than one person on the computer. Fixed. The menus keep temporary state somewhere private to each user rather than in a shared location. The translator shows no suggestions or examples The missing AI key . Translation itself still works. Other menu problems in the full file The translator returns a single word with no dictionary The flashcard tool builds a "},{"t":"Dictation","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#dictation","b":"It transcribes nonsense Your microphone volume was reset to a distorting level. Something resets it at every login, and a background service puts it back. If dictation was fine and suddenly got much worse, this is why. It is very slow A few seconds of speech taking ten or more means the engine was installed unoptimised. An install step rebuilds it about 13 times faster. That step warns and skips itself rather than failing, so it may have been skipped quietly. Live dictation types things I never said Usually repeated phrases. The engine does not cleanly separate one phrase from the next in live"},{"t":"Errors when you open a terminal","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#shell","b":"A wall of errors on every new terminal Unknown command, a temporary file that could not be created, a missing filename. All under Fish shell in the full file. The terminal multiplexer ignores all its add-ons tmux starts fine and quietly ignores every plugin line, so pane navigation and session-restore do nothing. Its add-on manager needs a one-time setup, which is an install step. Warnings when the desktop starts that are not problems Some startup messages mean nothing. The full file has an entry called \"What qtile.log looks like on a clean restart\" , the fastest way to tell a real error from "},{"t":"Finding the rest","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#full","b":"The complete record is TROUBLESHOOTING.md in the project folder: 186 entries, each with what you see, what caused it and what to do. Search it from the desktop: click the logo in the bar → Documentation → Troubleshooting . That list shows each entry by what you see rather than by a heading, so you can search the words you would actually type, and it jumps straight to the right place. Two other files, if you are digging deeper: File What it holds notes/archive/HANDOFF.md A short summary of what has been proven to work and what has not notes/archive/plan_found-but-not-fixed.md Everything that wa"},{"t":"Where everything lives","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html","b":"Which file to open to change something, and where to look when you want to know what happened."},{"t":"\"I want to change X\"","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#change","b":"You want to change Do this Colours Super + P then c . No file editing. See Changing the colours The wallpaper Super + P then w . Add your own images to ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/ How big everything is ui-scale-toggle . See the bar and the popups A keyboard shortcut ~/.config/qtile/config.py Which programs open on which workspace ~/.config/qtile/config.py , in the groups section What starts automatically when you log in ~/.config/qtile/autostart.sh The terminal ~/.config/kitty/ Notifications ~/.config/dunst/ Window shadows and animations ~/.config/picom/ The look of the menus ~/.config/rofi/ . They"},{"t":"How to edit a settings file safely","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#edit","b":"Edit the file. Check it before you rely on it: bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/validate.sh A couple of seconds. It loads the window manager settings for real rather than reading them, so a mistake is caught here instead of by the desktop disappearing. Apply it: Super + Shift + r The desktop restarts behind a frosted overlay. Your windows stay exactly where they were. Why step 2 matters. An error in the settings file does not produce an error message. The window manager silently loads its own plain built-in settings instead. The failure looks like your desktop turning into a stranger's, with no"},{"t":"The folder layout","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#layout","b":"Everything lives in one folder: ~/.dotfiles . ~/.dotfiles/ ├── .config/ all the settings │ ├── qtile/ the window manager, the popups, the shelf and updater │ ├── AtiScriptsV1/ the ~45 bundled commands │ ├── arch-config/ which software should be installed │ ├── eww/ the welcome tour and tooltips │ ├── rofi/ the menus │ ├── kitty/ dunst/ picom/ fish/ nvim/ qutebrowser/ ... │ └── secrets.env.example a template for your API keys ├── installScripts/ │ ├── install.sh the installer │ ├── wizard.sh its 47 steps │ ├── validate.sh the quick self-check │ ├── stow_script.sh puts settings files in place │ "},{"t":"Why editing the repo changes your desktop","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#symlinks","b":"The files in ~/.config/ are not copies. They are links back into ~/.dotfiles/.config/ . Editing either path edits the same file. So: You can edit whichever path you find easier. There is no \"apply my changes\" copy step to forget. Your changes join the project's history, so you can see what you changed and undo it. stow_script.sh sets this up. It runs during the install, and you can run it again by hand: bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/stow_script.sh Nothing is overwritten. Any file it would replace moves to ~/DefaultConfig/<timestamp>/ first. The details of what does and does not get linked Th"},{"t":"Adding and removing software","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#software","b":"The software list is written down in files rather than being whatever happens to be on the computer. A second computer can be made identical, and you can see what you added. Open the right file in ~/.dotfiles/.config/arch-config/modules/ and add or remove a line. File What belongs in it apps.yaml Everyday applications dev.yaml Programming tools media.yaml Sound and video fonts.yaml Fonts system-tools.yaml Command-line utilities wm.yaml The desktop itself network.yaml Wi-Fi and Bluetooth optional.yaml Things installed only on request — Docker, Java, printing Apply it: dcli sync A snapshot is ta"},{"t":"Where the logs are","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#logs","b":"File What it holds /tmp/wizard-<name>.err One file per install step that failed. Read these, not the summary card ~/.local/share/qtile/qtile.log The window manager's own log. Where to look when a shortcut errors /var/log/ati-os-firstboot.log The whole first-boot desktop build, if you installed from USB /tmp/qupdate-$UID-run.log The last background update ~/ati-os-build/logs/ One file per package, when building a USB stick Files the desktop generates for itself You never create or edit these. Knowing they exist explains a lot. File What it holds ~/.cache/qtile/theme_mode Which colour scheme is "},{"t":"Undoing things","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#undo","b":"To undo Run All the settings changes the installer made ./wizard.sh --uninstall Just one step ./wizard.sh --uninstall --only=<name> See what an undo would do first ./wizard.sh --uninstall --dry-run The startup screen specifically ./wizard.sh --uninstall --only=boot-splash Caps Lock behaving as Alt ./wizard.sh --uninstall --only=xmodmap Going back to the normal shell chsh -s /usr/bin/bash $USER A bad software update Restore a snapshot — see the recovery steps Undo never removes software and never deletes large downloads like the dictation models or the wallpapers. Settings changes only."},{"t":"The project's own documents","p":"Where everything lives","u":"reference.html#docs","b":"These live in ~/.dotfiles . Fastest way to read them: the logo in the bar → Documentation , which shows them formatted and read-only. File What it is README.md The overview and the install instructions TROUBLESHOOTING.md 186 real problems, each with cause and fix notes/archive/HANDOFF.md A short summary of what has been proven and what has not notes/archive/plan_found-but-not-fixed.md Everything looked at and deliberately left alone, with the reason. Some entries are correct as written and are recorded so nobody breaks them again installScripts/iso/README.md Building the USB stick installScrip"},{"t":"How it works","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html","b":"The reasoning behind the decisions. Nothing here is needed to use the desktop."},{"t":"The shape of the thing","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#shape","b":"The project splits in two, and it matters which half you are on. Half Portable? Why The configs and the ~45 bundled commands Any Linux Nothing is hardcoded to a distribution, username, monitor, GPU or resolution. Paths come from $HOME , the battery and webcam from /sys and v4l2, screens from live xrandr , DPI from the panel, fonts from fc-match The installer Arch only pacman , yay , the AUR, dcli , mkinitcpio , systemd-boot That second row is a deliberate limit. Hand-writing apt and dnf equivalents for 275 packages nobody has ever installed on those systems produces a config that looks portabl"},{"t":"The USB image","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#iso","b":"Script Job aur-repo.sh Builds 31 community packages in a clean isolated environment build-iso.sh Stages the profile, those packages and the dotfiles, then builds the image test-iso.sh Four-phase test in a virtual machine: bootloader, install, boot, filesystem The split is deliberate. Rebuilding 31 packages to change a boot-menu label would be absurd, so the first script's output is cached and the second only checks for it. Everything is written under ~/ati-os-build , never into the git checkout. On the author's machine / has about 6 GB free and /home has 100. The profile is a fork, kept close "},{"t":"The prebuilt software","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#prebuilt","b":"The USB route takes 20 minutes instead of two hours because four packages ship compiled. One of them, a statically-linked package manager kept as an emergency tool, is an hour on its own. The trust model Those packages are added to the new system as an unsigned source. That is defensible for one reason: the packages and the tool reading them arrive on the same read-only medium , so a signature would only verify the medium against itself. The source is removed again at the end of the install . The finished system is never left with a permanent unsigned software source. The packages are copied i"},{"t":"Why everything checks first","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#preflight","b":"Every entry point runs a preflight that names the value that failed rather than saying \"requirements not met\". Each check exists because that failure happened and was expensive to diagnose. Check The failure it prevents UEFI firmware Every boot-related step here is written against one bootloader. A second, untested way to boot is the worst kind to ship Secure Boot Not fatal at install time because it is fatal later and far more confusingly. The install completes, the machine reboots, the firmware silently refuses an unsigned bootloader, and the user sees \"no bootable device\" and blames the ins"},{"t":"The installer","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#wizard","b":"\"It never asks you anything\" is a guarantee install.sh is wizard.sh --yes . Every step runs with its input closed, and the wizard refuses to reach a prompt in that mode. This used to be untrue twice over. The run stopped dead at the end on a password prompt. And any step whose child process asked a question hung forever behind the spinner , showing nothing but an animation. Both are fixed. The installer also keeps admin permission alive for the whole run, refreshed in the background, so long builds do not silently drop package installs when the credential cache would expire mid-run. The step o"},{"t":"Why the username is the key","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#username","b":"Package sets and settings profiles are keyed off the username , not the hostname, even though the configuration field is called host: . It reads like a bug and cannot be renamed. That field belongs to dcli's schema, not this project's, and the string is compiled into the dcli binary. Renaming it would stop dcli finding its own configuration. The behaviour is also the one wanted. These are per-user package sets, and two accounts on one machine legitimately want different ones. It is documented in place so the next reader does not \"fix\" it."},{"t":"How the settings files get deployed","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#stow","b":"stow_script.sh wraps GNU stow , which links a folder of files into your home directory. Editing a file in the project immediately changes the live configuration, with no copy step to forget. Four things it does that a plain stow . does not: It derives its own location. The project is routinely checked out elsewhere, and hardcoding ~/.dotfiles made it silently deploy a different checkout than the one it was run from. It backs up before linking , into ~/DefaultConfig/<timestamp>/ . Only regular files are moved at the top level. An existing directory is not a conflict, and moving a real ~/.claude"},{"t":"How software is declared","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#packages","b":"Every package is declared in YAML and reconciled by dcli. Change a file, run dcli sync , and the system converges. A snapshot is taken every sync, so a broken update is a one-command rollback. Three files are never synced automatically File Why The three graphics-*.yaml Each is correct on exactly one class of machine. The gpu step reads live hardware ids and installs whichever matches. NVIDIA uses the source-built driver, not the prebuilt one, because the prebuilt module is built against the current kernel only. Booting the rescue entry would come up with no NVIDIA driver at all, which is prec"},{"t":"The boot chain","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#boot","b":"Why there is a rescue entry The boot-fallback step writes two boot entries for the long-term-support kernel: a normal one and a rescue one with every driver loaded. The kernel package alone ships none , so without this the rescue kernel is installed but unreachable. Both stay verbose. The step strips the quiet and splash options from them. A rescue entry that inherited the splash would show a logo while hiding the kernel messages saying what broke, which is indistinguishable from the failed boot you are escaping. The startup splash Arch boots with the kernel log on screen: a wall of scrolling "},{"t":"How one command recolours fifteen programs","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#theming","b":"There is no shared mechanism. Fifteen consumers, fifteen reload paths, because none of these programs agree on how to be recoloured. Consumer Reload mechanism kitty A live command per open window, plus a signal rofi A file swapped underneath it dunst Render a template, then restart qtile A restart, detached so the caller does not deadlock GTK 3 and 4 An overlay stylesheet imported from the cache Qt 5 and Qt 6 A 21-role palette file. Needs QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=qt6ct , exported at session start. Qt ignores the GTK theme entirely and falls back to a light palette without it. Read at application s"},{"t":"How it fits any screen","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#scaling","b":"Every pixel value was tuned on a 1366×768 14-inch panel. Copied unchanged to a 15-inch 4K laptop, that is a sliver of a bar with unreadable text. It is the one thing dotfiles cannot keep identical by copying files, because the right answer depends on the glass. ui-scale computes a factor from the display's real DPI. The window manager multiplies every font size, bar height, icon and margin by it, and a second setting carries the same factor into applications, menus and notifications. It runs at every login, so docking to an external monitor re-scales. Screen DPI Factor 14\" 1366×768 (reference)"},{"t":"The restart veil","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#veil","b":"Restarting the window manager normally means watching every window from every workspace flash across the screen for two seconds. That is unfixable from configuration alone. Its own startup maps every window before the hook that would let you intervene ever fires. So the restart raises a veil, in a separate process , so it survives the restart replacing the window manager itself. It frosts the desktop, shows a card with each window's real icon, and reports genuine progress from the incoming process rather than a timer. It pauses notifications and keeps itself on top by reacting to window-stacki"},{"t":"Why the popups are popups","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#popups","b":"The sound, monitor, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth pickers replaced four separate programs. Being drawn by the window manager means no window to manage, no workspace to put it on and no floating rule to write. They close when their mode ends. The tray icons stay as icons only. The cheatsheets scroll rather than page Paging tied capacity to size . The only way to show more was to be bigger, and they had grown to 1330×750 on a 1366×768 screen, covering the very window you opened them to ask about. With a viewport that moves, size only decides how much you see at once. Only one sheet is open at a time, whic"},{"t":"The documentation menu","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#docsmenu","b":"Left-clicking the logo used to open a terminal, which four other shortcuts already do. A desktop with this many shortcuts, themes and custom tools has a discovery problem, not a terminal-launching problem. So the most prominent click in the bar now answers \"what can this thing do\". Every section is generated from the live system , because a hand-written menu drifts the moment anything is added, and does it silently. The shortcut list parses the config's syntax tree , not a regular expression. Shortcuts nest several mode levels deep, and a regex pass found 44 of them while losing every mode pre"},{"t":"How this gets tested","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#testing","b":"Layer Time Catches validate.sh seconds Parses every tracked shell, Python, fish and YAML file. Loads the window manager config for real. Greps for hardcoded home paths, checks every template has a renderer, runs a full installer dry-run wizard.sh --audit seconds Declared packages against installed ones, both directions container-test.sh ~3 min A real install as a user who is not you. Whether running twice changes anything vm-test.sh --unattended ~2 hours A graphical session, system services, booting, the desktop The pre-commit hook runs the first two only. A hook that takes three minutes gets "},{"t":"The two lessons that cost the most","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#lessons","b":"1. On a clean install, the step that reports the failure is almost never the step at fault. One full disk presented as five broken steps, starting 32 steps after the cause. One blocked package transaction presented as two. Reading the per-step error logs is what separated five root causes from twenty-two symptoms. All five were invisible on the author's machine, because that machine was the single configuration where the code happened to work: Two packages providing the same virtual dependency. On a clean machine the resolver picks the wrong one, it conflicts with the declared one, and the ent"},{"t":"Things that look like bugs and are not","p":"How it works","u":"under-the-hood.html#quirks","b":"Recorded in the project's own notes/archive/plan_found-but-not-fixed.md so nobody re-breaks them. Looks wrong Why it is right host: keys off the username See above grub_boost.sh refuses rather than guessing It aborts on a trailing-comment or unquoted kernel command line instead of corrupting it, and prints the offending line. A few hand-edited files need a manual edit first The installer hard-fails on non-Arch Loosening the gate would let package-manager-specific steps run on a system that cannot satisfy them The reference boot entries hardcode a partition id and an Intel microcode file They a"}];
